From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 02:29:40 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: read coherent global memory through relaxed
 atomics

A compute kernel that reads device memory through a coherent pointer inside a loop miscompiles on macOS 27: the first such load in an iteration returns the right value and every later one returns zero. Two coherent loads are enough and one is not, and any unrelated edit moves the failure to a different kernel rather than removing it.

An application reaches this without asking for coherency, because glslang decorates a volatile buffer member Coherent as well as Volatile. A robust read of a volatile storage buffer is one bounds-checked load per component, so a vec4 element comes back as its first component followed by zeroes, which is neither the stored data nor the zeroes an out-of-bounds read owes it.

Perform a coherent 32-bit global access with atomic_load_explicit and atomic_store_explicit under memory_order_relaxed, one component at a time, instead of dereferencing a coherent device pointer. A relaxed device atomic is coherent at device scope by definition and the compiler cannot fold it. Components with no MSL atomic type, and the accesses the driver marks coherent itself, keep the pointer qualifier. Only a shader that has such an access declares the two helpers, so every other shader still translates byte for byte as before.

Record the issue as KK_WORKAROUND_17 and pin the emitted MSL for a coherent access, an ordinary one, and the disabled workaround.
